 timing-5. Residual gap: the nine small/medium workloads still sit 2.2-4.2 us above their references (0.01454-0.01659 vs 0.01155-0.01315 ms); measured separately, the raw cached launcher alone costs 9.07 us and torch's own xlogy_ submit is ~10.0 us, so the remaining wrapper overhead above the launch floor is roughly 5-6 us and is now the only source of loss.", "root_cause": "Confirmed the frozen hypothesis on both counts. The seed's fixed ~23 us per-call cost decomposed into ~9.1 us raw launch floor, ~4.7 us from the lazily resolved triton.cdiv, ~0.7 us stream query and the remainder in Python frames and per-call grid derivation; caching grid/tile/device in the entry tuple and inlining the fast path removed the removable part. The fp16/bf16 4096x4096 device deficit was tile width, not math precision: device-bound runs showed 152.3 us at BLOCK 4096 versus 92.3 us at BLOCK 8192 for fp16, with masked and unmasked loads and fp32 versus native-dtype math indistinguishable, so the larger contiguous DMA per program was the actual mechanism.", "round_num": 2, "strategy_evolution": "Round 1 localized the deficit to a constant per-call host cost; round 2 removed the dominant removable term (lazy triton.cdiv) plus widened the tile, reaching 0.8569. The bottleneck has narrowed to the difference between our ~9.1 us raw launcher floor and the reference's ~10-12 us full call, so round 3 targets only the wrapper operations that sit above that floor."}}
